---
const rows = [
  ["I remember the day when we moved.", "Describes the noun day.", "Relative clause"],
  ["When we moved, I was excited.", "Tells when I was excited.", "Time clause"],
  ["This is the park where we met.", "Describes the noun park.", "Relative clause"],
  ["I smiled because we met.", "Gives a reason for smiling.", "Reason clause"]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Clauses</h1>
  <p class="topic-lead">More about relative clauses</p>
  <div class="summary-stack">
    <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
      <h2>Identifying, or extra information?</h2>
      <p>
        “The book <strong>that has a red cover</strong> is mine.” The clause identifies which book. No commas.
      </p>
      <p>
        “My bike, <strong>which has a red frame</strong>, is outside.” The bike is already identified. The
        commas set off extra information. Do not comma every relative clause.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="summary-block summary-mint">
      <h2>Build one from two sentences</h2>
      <p>I met a scientist. The scientist studies volcanoes.</p>
      <p>I met a scientist <strong>who studies volcanoes</strong>.</p>
      <p>Ask which noun the clause describes, and what it adds about that noun.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <section class="summary-block summary-blue">
    <h2>Does the clause describe a noun?</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">What it does</th>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Type</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map(([example, job, type]) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{example}</td>
              <td>{job}</td>
              <td>{type}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <div class="trap-card trap-compact">
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>Whose, who’s, and leaving the word out</h2>
      <p>
        the child who’s coat was lost
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
        the child <strong>whose</strong> coat was lost
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Who’s</em> means who is or who has. You may omit the relative word when it is the object: “The
        cake <strong>Mum baked</strong>.” Do not omit it when it is the subject: “The cake
        <strong>that fell</strong>.”
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
